 1 big thing: Midterm food fight 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images The Cheesecake Factory is caught up in a midterm war of words after former President Obama invoked the restaurant chain to mock President Trump's White House makeover, Axios' Herb Scribner reports. 

 Obama's jab ignited a debate over class and cultural elitism, with conservatives accusing the former president of looking down on ordinary Americans. 🎤 Obama criticized Trump's changes to the White House during a rally in Ohio on Saturday for Democratic U.S. Senate candidate Sherrod Brown and gubernatorial candidate Amy Acton.

 Obama said: "I mean, I don't know if you've noticed, but he seems very focused on home renovations." "What's up with the gold? I mean, he's got the White House looking like Saddam Hussein opened a Cheesecake Factory." Trump responded yesterday during a rally in Mississippi, saying the Cheesecake Factory is "a successful company" and Obama "doesn't like successful companies."

 He added that Obama "doesn't understand the White House has to be clean, pristine, beautiful" and that it "can't have cracks in it." Several Trump administration officials and conservative commentators, including Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent, piled on. President Trump speaks to reporters in the Oval Office on Sept. 18. Photo: Chip Somodevilla/Getty Images 🖼️ The big picture: Obama's broader criticism was that Trump is prioritizing White House renovations over lowering costs for Americans — a major issue heading into the midterms.

 The Cheesecake Factory is known for ornate interiors, gold accents, decorative columns and enormous menus. 📱 Former Obama aides are relishing the GOP outrage. 

 Jon Favreau — who was Obama's head speechwriter and is now co-founder of Crooked Media and co-host of Pod Save America — tweeted : "Who could've guessed the joke would've baited Trump into spending the last week of the election talking more about his White House renovations?" Go deeper . 

 
 
